hypothesis.brief:
OPERATIONALIZED HYPOTHESIS (from the Socratic Council):
  Question:   If you delete a single central-metabolism enzyme, does the cell die, slow down, or just reroute around it?
  Claim (H1): Central-metabolism enzyme genes differ in essentiality: a minority (~15-25%) of single central-metabolism KOs are lethal/near-lethal (division_rate collapses, growth_rate -> 0 within 2-3 generations) because they sit at unbranched/bottleneck steps, while the majority (~75-85%) show only a moderate growth-rate penalty (10-40% reduction) as the network reroutes flux through paralogous or alternate pathways (e.g. glycolysis<->PPP<->ED shunt), rather than either uniformly killing the cell or leaving it unaffected.
  H1: Across a panel of single central-metabolism enzyme knockouts in basal glucose medium, the distribution of fitness outcomes (measured as division_rate and growth_rate relative to wildtype) is BIMODAL/heterogeneous: a minority subset (~15-25%) are near-lethal (division_rate < 0.2 of WT) with concurrent dry_mass/protein_mass accumulation ARRESTING (not merely fba_objective dropping), and the remainder show partial, reroute-mediated slowdown (growth_rate reduced 10-40% vs WT, division_rate > 0.7), not a uniform response.
  H0: All single central-metabolism enzyme KOs produce statistically indistinguishable outcomes from each other and from wildtype (growth_rate and division_rate within 2 SEM of WT for all genes), OR all KOs are uniformly lethal/uniformly mildly-reduced with no bimodal split (dip test fails to reject unimodality; fraction lethal not distinguishable from 0% or from 100%).
  Predicted effect: Bimodal fraction split: ~20% of tested central-metabolism gene KOs classified lethal (division_rate < 0.2xWT AND dry_mass/protein_mass plateau within 1 generation of division arrest), ~80% classified as reroute/slowdown (growth_rate reduced 10-40% vs WT, division_rate > 0.7xWT); dip-test statistic for bimodality across the growth_rate-reduction distribution significant at p<0.05, distinguishing from a unimodal (all-mild or all-lethal) distribution. Given panel size n=10 and ~20% expected lethal fraction (~2 genes), this is treated as a LOW-POWER exploratory screen: the dip-test and fraction estimates are point estimates with wide uncertainty, not a precise genome-wide claim. The mass-plateau cross-check is reported as a SECONDARY, conditional diagnostic (see auxiliary_assumptions) rather than a load-bearing falsifier on its own.
  Operationalize: 'cell death / lethality' -> division_rate (fraction of generations with successful division; division_rate < 0.2 of WT reference classified lethal)
  Operationalize: 'growth slowdown' -> growth_rate (instantaneous specific growth rate (1/h) relative to wildtype/basal mean, expressed as % reduction)
  Operationalize: 'metabolic rerouting' -> fba_objective and growth_rate recovery pattern (FBA objective value drop is smaller than the raw stoichiometric loss would predict, and growth_rate stabilizes above lethal threshold across generations (not monotonically declining))
  Operationalize: 'genuine metabolic collapse vs solver artifact' -> dry_mass and protein_mass (accumulation channels treated as the best-available cross-check on division_rate/fba_objective) (for genes classified lethal by division_rate/fba_objective=0, check whether dry_mass/protein_mass STILL increase across generations (mass-accumulation-without-division signature, whatever its causal origin) vs PLATEAU/decline (collapse signature: mass stalls concurrently with division arrest); interpretation of this check is conditioned on the auxiliary assumption below about how these channels are computed)
  Operationalize: 'central-metabolism enzyme panel' -> gene_knockout targets (a defined set of central-metabolism genes spanning glycolysis, TCA, PPP, and ED-shunt (e.g. pfkA, gapA, pgi, gltA, sdhA, zwf, edd, pgk, eno, pyk))
  Falsifier: disconfirm(target='gene_knockout/basal (panel, e.g. pfkA)', reference='wildtype/basal', channel='division_rate'); Screen >=10 central-metabolism gene KOs (5-6 seeds each, 3-4 generations); classify each as lethal (division_rate<0.2xWT) or non-lethal; apply Hartigan dip test for bimodality on the growth_rate-reduction distribution across the panel, reject unimodal null if p<0.05. As a secondary, non-decisive diagnostic, for genes classified lethal, additionally check whether dry_mass/protein_mass plateau (slope not significantly >0 vs WT, Welch t<2) or continue rising -- this diagnostic is reported alongside the primary result but does NOT by itself overturn the division_rate-based lethality classification, given the open uncertainty about whether these mass channels are computed independently of fba_objective in this simulator.; refuted if The dip test fails to reject unimodality (p>=0.05) with either 0/10 or 10/10 genes in the lethal class -- contradicting H1's heterogeneous-outcome claim. (The mass-plateau diagnostic, if it shows continued mass rise for all 'lethal' genes, is recorded as a flagged caveat on interpretation of those specific genes' collapse mechanism, not as an independent refutation of the overall bimodality claim.)
  Rival: Uniform robustness: E. coli central metabolism is so redundant that essentially NO single central-enzyme KO is lethal in this model; all show mild slowdown only. -> distinguished by: 0/10 (or near-0) genes classified lethal; division_rate stays >0.7xWT for the entire panel; dip test non-significant with all mass at low growth_rate-reduction
  Rival: Uniform fragility: central metabolism is a single connected chokepoint network with no effective rerouting in this simulator, so most/all single KOs are lethal. -> distinguished by: >=8/10 genes classified lethal (division_rate<0.2xWT); dip test non-significant with all mass at high growth_rate-reduction/near-zero division
  Rival: Apparent lethality is actually a division-machinery/solver artifact (FBA infeasibility mechanically zeroing growth_rate as its derived biomass flux) rather than true metabolic collapse, since growth_rate is typically computed FROM fba_objective and thus the two cannot be used to cross-validate each other. -> distinguished by: For genes classified lethal, dry_mass and protein_mass continue to rise at a WT-comparable rate despite division_rate collapsing and fba_objective=0 -- flagged as a caveat on those genes' interpretation, but this rival is primarily addressed by the fact that division_rate itself (the PRIMARY classification channel) is a distinct, directly-measured cell-cycle readout, not a re-derivation of fba_objective, so a solver artifact in fba_objective alone cannot manufacture a division_rate collapse without an independent division-machinery failure
